This YANI strives to remove two things about the quest I find not so cool:
There is a call that your help is needed ("Look for a ...ic transporter"). 
When going through that portal, you are presented a description of how bad 
things stand at your home. However:
 1) You enter the home level and find many defenders, many attackers - but
    no action at all. Shouldn't there be a fierce battle going on?
 2) Despite the urgency implied by both call for help and description, you
    can take as you long like before tackling the home level.

The first point has been addressed by Nephi's Grudge patch which I always 
use. But I would like to go further. This is my proposal:

 Get a reward from the quest leader if you clean the home level from
 attackers. The reward should depend in a major way on

 + how many guardians (the friendlies) survived
 + swiftness 1: number of turns spent on home level (the smaller the better)
 - use of genocide on main enemy monsters (eg. troll,ogre,T,O for barbarians)

 and in a minor way on

 + swiftness 2: number of turns since hero heard help call for the first time


Advantages:
 You can completely ignore it and nothing has changed.
 But if you feel like it, use everything available (all those wands,
 stinking clouds, boulders, healing etc.) to fulfill the task really good.
 [This is what I consider the cool part!] It should be like an optional
 extra challenge; fitting atmosphere and story as well as gameplay.

 What's described under swiftness 2 deals with problem 2) above. With this
 YANI one would get some bonus for taking the homeland's problem serious.

The reward:
 Shouldn't be too good but must be variable to depend on performance.

 One idea: Possibly the leader could enchant some of your stuff. Say, up 
  to three items (one wielded weapon, one worn armour piece, one ring) by 
  at most two.

 Another idea: The surviving guardians build a new portal from the home 
  level to some place in the dungeon. Decree that the _maximal_ number of 
  stair climbs saved (important for the amulet run) is about eight dungeon 
  levels.


Necessary tweaks:
 Perhaps grudge needs an overhaul to provide better fighting. (As it is 
 now, grudging monsters will not go out of their way to attack each other.)

 Give verbal descriptions after #chats with the leader (e.g.
 "Good to see you! But some [monsters] still dwell here and do harm." or
 "You have been very brave! We will reward you for saving so many lives.")

 Of course the whole thing is independent of experience levels. So maybe
 characters with XL<14 shouldn't get expelled to the main dungeon but just 
 told that they're not ready (so they can hunt down quest enemies further).

 Perhaps there should be some random pits to make cockatricing a bit risky.


Problems:
 You will notice that there cannot be a common rewarding scheme for all
 classes. Thus we should define the difficulty for all classes separately.
 This could be done by giving weights to the objective. Three examples:

 Ranger - here it is even difficult to get to the leader in one go. And
   enemies are hard (lots of centaurs) and can fight guardians at the end 
   of a long spiral path. Hence give mainly points for speed and additional 
   points for survivors.
 Barbarian - here the enemies consist of ogres and trolls but the layout is 
   open. Therefore you have to fight a long time until they're all dead and 
   gone but in the meantime you can do a lot to help/protect the guardians. 
   Hence give mainly points for survivors and additional points for speed.
 Archeologist - here the enemies are wimpy (only snakes and mummies); even
   simpler, the guardians are safely divided from them (they reside in a 
   closed building). Hence this is a very easy class (for this task) and 
   regardless how points are distributed, return only a small reward.

 (There is a problem with the storyline: the quest leader must be exempted 
 from grudge, in order to keep the quest winnable. But why should the 
 enemies spare just him?)

